{
 "study_id": "cyber-rename",
 "title": "Attack-model planning under neutralized vocabulary (refusal-sensitivity)",
 "purpose": "The same two real CVE attack/defence formal models (Log4Shell CVE-2021-44228; Struts/Equifax CVE-2017-5638) are posed THREE ways per question, with identical structure (stages, preconditions, branches, blockers) and shared Z3 ground truth: (1) attackmodel — the original security vocabulary with the named CVE; (2) logistics — every noun renamed into a neutral logistics / office-relocation vocabulary; (3) abstract — a semantically-empty control vocabulary (s1..s6, operators, toggles) with no domain and no named-CVE anchor; (4) anchored — the identical abstract body with ONE defensive-framing paragraph added (no CVE, no attack nouns). The experiment measures whether a frontier model's refusals track the vocabulary rather than the structure; the abstract arm separates a loaded rename lexicon and the deleted CVE anchor from renaming per se, and the abstract->anchored pair isolates the legitimacy anchor as a single variable. A refusal-sensitivity control, not a capability ranking.",
